Container exits immediately|A newly started container stops before serving traffic.|docker logs --tail 100 CONTAINER|Inspect exit code, effective entrypoint and logs. Check required environment, executable paths and architecture rather than repeatedly restarting.|Correct the image command or required runtime configuration and recreate from the reviewed image. If a deployment introduced the failure, use the known-compatible image as mitigation.|Confirm the container remains running, serves a real request and handles graceful stop correctly.
Published port still unreachable|A host port mapping exists but clients cannot connect.|docker port CONTAINER|Compare the host bind address, container port and application's listener inside the container. EXPOSE alone does not publish a port.|Correct the mapping or application bind address and recreate with the intended limited exposure. Change firewall rules only if path evidence requires it.|Test from the intended client network and verify unintended networks do not gain access.
Data disappears after replacement|Application data is missing after recreating a container.|docker inspect --format '{{json .Mounts}}' CONTAINER|Determine whether data lived in the old writable layer, a different named volume or an unexpected bind source. Preserve the old container and volumes while investigating.|Recover from the correct retained data source or backup and mount durable storage explicitly. Correct deployment configuration so replacements reuse the intended data.|Create and read representative data across a controlled replacement and verify backup restoration separately.
Bind-mount permission failure|A non-root container cannot write to a mounted host directory.|docker inspect CONTAINER|Compare the container UID/GID with host ownership, ACLs and security labeling. Confirm the mount is writable and points to the intended path.|Grant the application identity only the required rights or adjust ownership during provisioning. Avoid privileged mode or broad world-write permissions as shortcuts.|Test writes as the runtime identity and verify unrelated host paths remain inaccessible.
Memory-driven restart loop|The container repeatedly exits under peak traffic.|docker stats --no-stream|Correlate memory growth with exit state, OOM evidence and configured limits. Distinguish a leak from a predictable concurrency peak.|Roll back a memory regression, bound concurrency or adjust justified capacity. A restart policy alone cannot fix insufficient memory.|Run representative load and confirm memory stabilizes and restart counts stop increasing without host pressure.
Compose starts before dependency ready|The web container fails on boot even though the database container was started first.|docker compose ps -a|Inspect dependency health and application connection errors. Start ordering is not proof of database readiness.|Add appropriate readiness handling and bounded application retries; use supported Compose health conditions for startup where useful.|Recreate in a safe environment and confirm startup succeeds with realistic database warm-up and later dependency restarts.
Unexpected old image|A deployment appears to use old behavior even after a tag was updated.|docker image inspect IMAGE|Compare the running container's image ID with the expected digest and registry reference. A mutable tag and a locally cached image can diverge.|Pull and deploy the intended verified digest through the normal release process. Record digest-to-commit mapping instead of relying only on tag names.|Inspect the running container identity and verify the expected feature and rollback artifact.
Container ignores shutdown|Stopping a container waits for the timeout and then forces termination.|docker inspect CONTAINER|Inspect PID-one behavior and shell wrappers; determine whether signals reach the application and whether it drains work.|Use exec-form startup or a suitable init/wrapper that forwards signals and reaps children. Implement bounded graceful shutdown in the application.|Send a controlled stop during test work and verify graceful exit, completed or safely retried requests and no zombie buildup.
Host storage consumed by Docker|The daemon host is running out of disk space.|docker system df|Separate image/cache growth from container logs and durable volumes. Identify owners and retention before deleting any resource.|Apply scoped cleanup to confirmed disposable resources and configure log/cache retention. Do not prune volumes just because they are currently detached.|Confirm reclaimed space and successful application data access; monitor growth after the next build and log cycle.
Secret accidentally baked into image|A credential was copied during build and deleted in a later Dockerfile step.|docker image history IMAGE|Inspect the build process and layer history; later deletion does not remove earlier layer contents. Assess registry and artifact access.|Rotate the exposed credential, rebuild without the secret using a proper secret mechanism and remove affected artifacts under policy.|Verify the replacement image and build logs contain no secret material and consumers use the rotated credential.
